## Test Anti-Pattern Audit: `ProductCatalogTests.cs`

This test file is a textbook example of **"coverage theater"** — every production method is invoked (giving 95%+ line coverage) but almost nothing is actually verified. Here's the severity-ranked list:

---

### 🔴 CRITICAL — Tests That Verify Nothing

**1. `AddProduct_Works` (line 17)** — Fire-and-forget, zero assertions.
```csharp
// FIX: verify the product was persisted
_catalog.AddProduct(product);
var retrieved = _catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001");
Assert.AreEqual("Widget", retrieved!.Name);
Assert.AreEqual(9.99m, retrieved.Price);
```

**2. `UpdatePrice_Works` (line 32)** — Calls `UpdatePrice` but never checks the new price.
```csharp
// FIX: assert the price actually changed
_catalog.UpdatePrice("SKU-001", 14.99m);
Assert.AreEqual(14.99m, _catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001")!.Price);
```

**3. `RemoveProduct_Works` (line 38)** — Calls delete, never verifies absence.
```csharp
// FIX: assert the product is gone
_catalog.RemoveProduct("SKU-001");
Assert.IsNull(_catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001"));
```

**4. `ApplyBulkDiscount_Works` (line 68)** — Applies 10% discount, never checks math.
```csharp
// FIX: verify discounted prices
_catalog.ApplyBulkDiscount(0.10m);
Assert.AreEqual(8.991m, _catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001")!.Price);
Assert.AreEqual(17.991m, _catalog.GetProduct("SKU-002")!.Price);
```

**5. `GetProductCount_Works` (line 63)** — Doesn't even assert the count is 0.
```csharp
// FIX: assert on the actual count
Assert.AreEqual(0, _catalog.GetProductCount());
_catalog.AddProduct(new Product("SKU-001", "Widget", 9.99m));
Assert.AreEqual(1, _catalog.GetProductCount());
```

**6. `ImportFromCsv_Works` (line 85)** — Imports data, never checks it landed.
```csharp
// FIX: verify the imported product
_catalog.ImportFromCsv("SKU-001,Widget,9.99");
var p = _catalog.GetProduct("SKU-001");
Assert.AreEqual("Widget", p!.Name);
Assert.AreEqual(9.99m, p.Price);
```

---

### 🟠 HIGH — Weak Assertions (Assert.IsNotNull on non-nullable paths)

**7. `GetProduct_Works` (line 25)** — `IsNotNull` only proves a record exists; doesn't verify correctness.
```csharp
// FIX: assert field values
Assert.AreEqual("SKU-001", result!.Sku);
Assert.AreEqual(9.99m, result.Price);
```

